<Project>

  <!--
    Inherited by every project under this directory, so the C# ruleset in
    .editorconfig applies to the whole solution from the first project onward
    (ADR-0065).

    This file exists because severity is not enough. Measured on 2026-08-02
    against .NET SDK 10.0.301: an .editorconfig carrying
    `dotnet_diagnostic.IDE1006.severity = error` and
    `dotnet_diagnostic.IDE0055.severity = error`, against a file violating both,
    produced `Build succeeded` and exit 0. Setting the property below turned the
    same build into `Build FAILED` and exit 1. ADR-0065's "set to error severity
    so a violation fails the build" is therefore a statement about the pair, and
    landing the .editorconfig without this file would have shipped a ruleset that
    reads as enforced and is not.

    Until 2026-08-02 this was deliberately one property, because the rest had no
    projects to configure. The first project landed that day and brought the
    target-framework knob with it (ADR-0030 parameter C). MinVer and the
    reproducible-build stack are still ADR-0030's and still absent, and the
    public-API analyzers are still ADR-0044's.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    RS0017 at error (ADR-0044 parameter A), landed 2026-08-02.

    Its two siblings, RS0016 and RS0037, are set in .editorconfig where a
    severity belongs. This one is here because .editorconfig cannot reach it,
    which was found by measurement rather than expected.

    A diagnostic's severity is matched against the file it is REPORTED IN, and
    RS0017's subject is a stale line in the API text file, so it is reported at
    `PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt(N,1)` and not in any `.cs`. Measured 2026-08-02 on
    SDK 10.0.301 with analyzer 5.6.0, four placements were tried and four left
    it at its default of warning: `[*.cs]`, a section naming the two API files
    explicitly, `[*]`, and a root `.globalconfig` added through an MSBuild item.
    A `.globalconfig` in the project's own directory did work, and is rejected
    because it is per-project and would have to be copied into every package.
    This property is one line, inherited, and measured to exit 1.

    Do not "tidy" this into .editorconfig for symmetry. The symmetry is the bug.

    Why RS0017 specifically has to bite: it is the only one of the three that
    fires on a pure removal, a public member deleted from the code with its
    lines left in the API file. Measured while it was still a warning, that
    produced `2 Warning(s)`, `Build succeeded`, exit 0, which is the
    MAJOR-breaking direction of ADR-0044 parameter B passing a gate that read
    as configured.

    The $(WarningsAsErrors) prefix preserves anything a project or a future
    property sets, so this appends rather than replaces. TreatWarningsAsErrors
    is still NOT set, and this is not the beginning of it: this promotes one
    named diagnostic that an accepted ADR already calls an error.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);RS0017</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The target-framework knob (ADR-0030 parameter C).

    TWO properties, not one, and the reason is parameter B rather than taste: a
    published library multi-targets the current and next LTS while the host is
    single-target on the current one. Both read net10.0 today because .NET 12 has
    not shipped, so the difference is invisible and the shape is the only thing
    that can be got right in advance. When 12 ships, the library property becomes
    `net10.0;net12.0` and the application property does not move.

    LangVersion is deliberately ABSENT even though parameter C names it, and this
    is the one deviation in this file. Measured on SDK 10.0.301 with no
    Directory.Build.props at all, a net10.0 project already reports
    LangVersion 14.0: the default is derived from the target framework, so the
    knob above already centralizes it. Writing it here could only restate that
    default, which rots against the SDK, or set `latest`, which would make the
    language version float with whatever SDK is installed and break the very
    coupling to the target framework that parameter C wants. This applies
    ADR-0065's "write only what deviates" rule, which that ADR states about
    .editorconfig, to a second file. Recorded in ADR-0030 rather than only here.

    Nullable is enabled because it is load-bearing rather than tidy: design 23
    section 3 annotates its definition model with `string?` on some members and
    not others, and that distinction means nothing to the compiler unless this is
    on. ADR-0044 parameter A also puts nullable annotations in the public API
    file, which needs the same. Measured default: off.

    ImplicitUsings is enabled on the design corpus's foundations task 1.4, which
    is the only source that states it. Measured default: off.

    TreatWarningsAsErrors is NOT set. The corpus task 1.4 asks for it, no ADR
    does, and turning it on in the same change that lands the first project would
    make every future warning a build break before anyone has seen what warnings
    this codebase produces. It is a separate decision with a separate measurement,
    and ADR-0092's `_warnaserror` globalconfig item is adjacent to it. Measured
    default: false.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks>net10.0</NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks>
    <NamiApplicationTargetFramework>net10.0</NamiApplicationTargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
